-\section{Problem Formulation}
-\label{sec:problem}
-%======================================================================
-We now state the problem introduced in Section~\ref{sec:intro} precisely. Given a small dataset $D$, its schema $S$ (the features and their types) and a requested number of synthetic records $N$, the goal is to produce, without training a generator on $D$, a synthetic dataset $G$ that satisfies six requirements: it has exactly $N$ records (R1); every record conforms to $S$ (R2); the cost of producing it is measured (R3); it is faithful to the distribution of $D$ (R4); it does not reproduce the records of $D$ (R5); and adding it to the training data improves, or at least does not degrade, the classifiers trained on $D$ (R6). These requirements are not new; they combine two established lines of work. R1 to R3 correspond to data-quality dimensions used in data management, namely completeness, conformity to a schema and the cost of acquiring the data~\cite{wang1996beyond,batini2009methodologies}, and they concern the synthetic dataset as a data product: a system can guarantee them. R4 to R6 are the three dimensions on which synthetic data are evaluated in the literature, namely fidelity, privacy and utility~\cite{qian2023synthcity,lautrup2025syntheval}, and they concern the value of the synthetic records: they can only be measured. The relation between the two groups is therefore one of order. A dataset that fails R1 or R2 cannot be used at all, so its fidelity, privacy and utility are not even defined, whereas a dataset that satisfies R1 to R3 can then be judged on R4 to R6. Formally, let $D^{\mathrm{tr}}$ and $D^{\mathrm{te}}$ be a training and a test partition of a small dataset $D$ with schema $S$, and let a configuration $\theta$ of the system (LLM, context strategy, active rules) produce a synthetic dataset $G_\theta$ from $D^{\mathrm{tr}}$ without access to $D^{\mathrm{te}}$. Requirements R1 and R2 define the feasible set $\mathcal{F}(S,R,N)$ of datasets with exactly $N$ records that conform to $S$ and to the active rules $R$. The other requirements are objectives: a fidelity loss $L_{\mathrm{fid}}(D^{\mathrm{tr}},G_\theta)$ for R4, a privacy risk $L_{\mathrm{priv}}(D^{\mathrm{tr}},G_\theta)$ for R5, the change in downstream utility $\Delta U(G_\theta)$ for R6 and the generation cost $\kappa(\theta)$, which R3 requires to be measured and which should also be kept low:
-\begin{equation}
