 \section{Conclusion}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
 %======================================================================
-Small clinical datasets pose a dilemma for synthetic data generation. Generators fitted to the available records cannot add the information those records lack, whereas an LLM can bring knowledge from pre-training but returns text rather than a dataset. \system resolves the dilemma with a control layer that turns LLM responses into complete, validated datasets of the requested size and records their cost, without training on the target data. On six small healthcare datasets, direct prompting never delivered the requested dataset, while \system completes it by construction, at 1.9\% more tokens; with the larger, more recent LLM it gave the largest and most consistent F1 gain of five generators, although no generator was significantly better on every dataset. Because repeated runs of the same pipeline vary as much as most design choices, we recommend that SDG for small data be evaluated against such a noise floor. Future work will evaluate locally served LLMs, class-aware context selection and the deployment across healthcare facilities outlined in Section~\ref{sec:deployment}.
+Small healthcare datasets pose a dilemma for synthetic data generation: generators fitted to the available records can only recombine the patterns those records contain, whereas an LLM can add domain knowledge but returns text rather than a dataset. \system resolves the dilemma with a control layer that turns the responses of an LLM into complete synthetic datasets with the columns and types of the real data and a recorded cost, without training on the target data. On six public healthcare datasets, direct prompting never delivered the configured dataset, while \system did at 1.9\% more cost, and with the strongest LLM it gave the largest and most stable F1 gain of five generators. Because repeated runs of the same pipeline vary as much as most design choices, we recommend that SDG for small data be evaluated on repeated runs. Future work will evaluate locally served LLMs, class-aware context selection, privacy filters and the deployment across healthcare facilities outlined in Section~\ref{sec:deployment}.
 
